findings.body:
The server runs in the CLI's own event loop, but the browser is launched synchronously:

https://github.com/AlexisBalayre/pupitre/blob/e919e465e68c92e244af51698e4283409614157c/src/cli/index.ts#L1326-L1331

```ts
if (!opts.open) return;
try {
  execFileSync(process.platform === 'darwin' ? 'open' : 'xdg-open', [dashboard.url]);
} catch {
```

`execFileSync`/`spawnSync` uses `pipe` stdio by default. It returns only once the child has exited and every stdio pipe has reached EOF, so a grandchild that inherits the pipe keeps the call blocked.

On Linux, `xdg-open` either runs the browser in the foreground (generic or `$BROWSER` mode) or hands off through `gio`/`kde-open`. In both cases a browser that wasn't already running inherits those pipes and holds them open while it runs. Until it exits:
- the browser's request for `/?token=…` gets no answer, so the tab spins;
- the poll timer stops;
- the SIGINT/SIGTERM handlers can't run.

macOS `open` and an already-running browser return at once, which is likely why this went unnoticed. `pup report` and `pup map` use the same call (L2042, L2070), but nothing in those processes has to keep serving afterwards.

Fix: launch without blocking, e.g.

```ts
spawn(process.platform === 'darwin' ? 'open' : 'xdg-open', [dashboard.url], { detached: true, stdio: 'ignore' })
  .on('error', () => console.log('Could not open a browser; open the url above.'))
  .unref();
```

This needs `spawn` added to the `node:child_process` import, and the CLI tests that mock `execFileSync` updated to match.
